<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aqueduct of Los Milagros</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Acueducto de los Milagros</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">Aqueduct</a> of Los Milagros is a Roman aqueduct at Mérida in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emerita-augusta">Emerita Augusta</a>, capital of Lusitania. It carried water from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persephone">Proserpina</a> reservoir to the city, and a stretch of some 800 metres of its granite and brick piers still stands, up to about 25 metres high. Its name, 'the miracles', reflects local wonder at its survival, and it is part of Mérida's World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aqueduct-of-los-milagros.jpg" alt="Aqueduct of Los Milagros" width="320" height="246" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: doalex · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El_acueducto_de_Los_Milagros.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
